    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Epilepsy of unknown type on lamotrigine (G40.909)</content>. Maintenance dose reached without rash. The one seizure came early in the titration, at a low dose and after sleep loss. Trough level 6.2 ug/mL at steady state, recorded as his reference level. Sleep-deprived EEG normal. [S]<list><item>Continue lamotrigine 100 mg twice daily.</item><item>Keep the seizure diary.</item><item>No writing through the night.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gambling disorder (F63.0)</content>. Abstinent for nine weeks, engaged in therapy and peer support. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly therapy and Gamblers Anonymous.</item><item>Keep the self-exclusion and the bank block in place.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Seizure frequency, seizure diary, and seizure description</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One seizure in week three of the titration after sleep loss; none since reaching 150 mg a day</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine adverse effects (dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness, sleep, mood)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness, or sleep change</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Rash and mouth-sore check (lamotrigine)</td><td>Every contact during titration and the first two months, then with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No rash or mouth sores</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Contact at each lamotrigine dose step to check for rash</td><td>At each dose increase during titration</td><td>Deferred</td><td>No contact was scheduled during the six-week titration, a deviation from the plan to check for rash at each dose step. He knew to stop and call for any rash, and none occurred, so no harm resulted. Corrective action: a nurse telephone check at each step of any future titration</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Adherence and missed doses</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No missed doses; pill organizer and phone alarms</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Lamotrigine trough level</td><td>Once at steady state as a reference, then with breakthrough seizures, suspected nonadherence, or interacting medicines</td><td>Completed today</td><td>6.2 ug/mL trough at steady state; recorded as his reference level</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>EEG (routine, then sleep-deprived if normal)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Sleep-deprived EEG six weeks ago normal, with no epileptiform discharges</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Trigger review: sleep loss, alcohol, overwork, missed doses</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>The titration seizure followed a night without sleep; he now stops writing by midnight</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure safety and driving counseling</td><td>At diagnosis and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not driving; precautions followed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written seizure action plan for the people around him</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Used by a friend during the seizure in March</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, then every two to four months while symptoms are present</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-9 6, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review and safety plan, with the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit (gambling disorder and antiseizure-medicine labeling)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Gambling since the last visit: days, money lost, and urges (G-SAS for the past week)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No gambling for nine weeks; G-SAS 14 for the past week, from urges only</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for gambling disorder</td><td>Weekly sessions for at least 8 to 12 weeks, then as needed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Weekly, with every session attended</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Self-exclusion from casinos and online gambling</td><td>At the start of treatment; renew and extend after any lapse</td><td>Up to date</td><td>In place</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Financial safeguards (bank gambling block, co-signer for large withdrawals, limited cash)</td><td>At the start of treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Bank block and co-signer in place</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Peer support (Gamblers Anonymous)</td><td>Offered at the start of treatment and after any lapse</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two Gamblers Anonymous meetings attended</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Debt counseling</td><td>Once, early in treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen; repayment plan being drawn up</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicine for gambling disorder (opioid antagonist)</td><td>Considered if psychological treatment alone is not enough</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Responding to psychological treatment</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reach the lamotrigine maintenance dose without rash by the two-month visit. Outcome by 1865-05-05: Maintenance dose of 100 mg twice daily reached after six weeks; no rash [S]</item><item>No seizures for six months after reaching the maintenance dose. In progress; target 1865-10-06 [S]</item><item>Casino and online self-exclusion and a bank gambling block in place by the four-week visit. Outcome by 1865-03-03: Self-exclusion from casinos in the state and from licensed online sites, and a gambling block on his bank cards, in place [S]</item><item>No gambling through the eight-month visit. In progress; target 1865-10-06 [S]</item><item>Written seizure action plan, safety counseling, and a SUDEP discussion completed by the four-week visit. Outcome by 1865-03-03: Plan written and shared with two friends; SUDEP discussed with the neurologist [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults: Report of the Guideline Development Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology and the American Epilepsy Society (2015); Practice guideline update summary: Efficacy and tolerability of the new antiepileptic drugs I: Treatment of new-onset epilepsy (2018); Psychological therapies for pathological and problem gambling (2012); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, depression, bipolar, and mood disorders monograph (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-month review. Call the same day for a rash or a seizure. Next scheduled visit 1865-07-07: four-month review of seizure control, mood, and gambling recovery. [S]</paragraph></text>

